[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Common Data Structures Overview"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial provides an overview of fundamental data structures: Arrays, Linked Lists, Stacks, Queues, Hash Tables, Trees, and Graphs. Understanding these structures is crucial for efficient data management and algorithm design."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Arrays"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Arrays are contiguous blocks of memory used to store elements of the same data type. They offer constant-time access to elements using their index (O(1)). However, inserting or deleting elements in the middle of an array can be inefficient (O(n)) as it requires shifting subsequent elements."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Array in JavaScript
let myArray = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
console.log(myArray[2]); // Output: 3"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Linked Lists"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Linked lists are collections of nodes, where each node contains data and a pointer (or link) to the next node in the sequence. Unlike arrays, linked lists do not require contiguous memory allocation. Insertion and deletion operations are efficient (O(1)) if the node to be modified is known. Accessing an element by its position requires traversing the list (O(n))."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Linked List Node in JavaScript
class Node {
  constructor(data) {
    this.data = data;
    this.next = null;
  }
}
"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Stacks"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Stacks are linear data structures that follow the Last-In, First-Out (LIFO) principle. The primary operations are 'push' (add an element to the top) and 'pop' (remove the element from the top). Stacks are commonly used in function call management, expression evaluation, and undo/redo mechanisms."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Stack in JavaScript
let stack = [];
stack.push(1); // Add element to the top
stack.push(2);
console.log(stack.pop()); // Remove and return the top element (Output: 2)"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Queues"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Queues are linear data structures that follow the First-In, First-Out (FIFO) principle. Elements are added to the rear (enqueue) and removed from the front (dequeue). Queues are used in task scheduling, print queues, and breadth-first search algorithms."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Queue in JavaScript
let queue = [];
queue.push(1); // Add element to the rear
queue.push(2);
console.log(queue.shift()); // Remove and return the front element (Output: 1)"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Hash Tables"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Hash tables (or hash maps) are data structures that store key-value pairs. They use a hash function to compute an index (or hash code) for each key, which determines the location where the value is stored. Ideally, hash tables provide average-case constant-time complexity (O(1)) for insertion, deletion, and retrieval operations. However, in the worst case (e.g., many keys map to the same index), the complexity can degrade to O(n)."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Hash Table in JavaScript
let hashTable = {};
hashTable['name'] = 'John';
hashTable['age'] = 30;
console.log(hashTable['name']); // Output: John"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Trees"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Trees are hierarchical data structures consisting of nodes connected by edges. A tree has a root node, and each node can have zero or more child nodes. Binary trees are a common type of tree where each node has at most two children (left and right). Trees are used in various applications, including representing hierarchical relationships, implementing search algorithms (e.g., binary search trees), and parsing expressions."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Binary Tree Node in JavaScript
class TreeNode {
  constructor(data) {
    this.data = data;
    this.left = null;
    this.right = null;
  }
}
"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Graphs"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Graphs are non-linear data structures consisting of nodes (vertices) and edges that connect pairs of nodes. Graphs can be directed (edges have a direction) or undirected (edges have no direction). They are used to model relationships between objects, such as social networks, transportation networks, and dependencies between tasks. Common graph algorithms include breadth-first search (BFS), depth-first search (DFS), and shortest path algorithms (e.g., Dijkstra's algorithm)."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"// Example: Graph representation using adjacency list in JavaScript
let graph = {
  'A': ['B', 'C'],
  'B': ['A', 'D', 'E'],
  'C': ['A', 'F'],
  'D': ['B'],
  'E': ['B', 'F'],
  'F': ['C', 'E']
};
"}]
